What were the distinctive features of the Assyrian writing system, and how did it differ from other ancient writing systems?
The Assyrian writing system, specifically the cuneiform script, had several distinctive features. It was a syllabic and logographic system, meaning that it used a combination of symbols to represent both sounds and words. This system allowed for a "mixed" method of writing, where some symbols were used as logograms (representing words) and others as phonetic characters (representing sounds). This complexity bears a resemblance to the Old Japanese writing system.

In comparison to other ancient writing systems, the Assyrian cuneiform script was notable for its use of wedge-shaped marks on clay tablets, made by a blunt reed stylus. This script was adapted from the Sumerian script and was used to write the Akkadian, Assyrian, and Hittite languages.

The Assyrian writing system differed from other ancient writing systems in its use of a combination of logograms and phonetic characters, as well as its distinctive wedge-shaped marks. This system allowed for a high degree of expressiveness and complexity, making it well-suited to the needs of the ancient Assyrian civilization.
