At the time of his speech at the Cotton States and International Exposition in 1895, Washington was likely aware of the complex power dynamics at play in the South, where African-Americans were subject to racial segregation and limited rights. His speech, "The Atlanta Compromise," was an attempt to find a middle ground between accommodation and resistance, advocating for education and economic empowerment for African-Americans while acknowledging the existing social and political order.

Washington's relationships with key figures and organizations, such as the Tuskegee Institute and the philanthropists who supported him, likely influenced his speech and intended audience. He may have been seeking to appeal to a predominantly white audience, such as the one at the Cotton States and International Exposition, while also maintaining the support of his black constituents.

