What are the key differences between this technique and other methods of measuring brain activity, such as functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI)?
EEG-fMRI measures both electrical brain activity (via EEG) and haemodynamic changes (via fMRI), whereas traditional fMRI only measures haemodynamic changes. This allows EEG-fMRI to provide a more comprehensive understanding of brain activity by correlating electrical activity with associated blood flow changes.
