What is the history of daylight saving time in Australia, including its introduction and implementation dates?
Daylight saving time was first introduced in Australia during World War I. It was reintroduced in Tasmania in 1967 and then in other states, including New South Wales, Victoria, Queensland, South Australia, and the Australian Capital Territory, in 1971. Western Australia and the Northern Territory did not adopt daylight saving time initially. Queensland abandoned it in 1972. The implementation dates varied across states, with Tasmania starting on the first Sunday in October and the other states beginning on the last Sunday in October and ending on the last Sunday in March until 2008. From 2008/09, daylight saving was extended by four weeks.
