The creation of the Federal Reserve System was a response to a series of financial panics, particularly the panic of 1907, which led to calls for a central agency to provide liquidity and stability to the banking system. The Federal Reserve Act of 1913 was enacted on December 23, 1913, creating the Federal Reserve System as a central banking system for the United States. The Act established a system of regional Federal Reserve Banks and a seven-member governing board, and required all national banks to join the system. The Federal Reserve Act granted the Federal Reserve authority to issue Federal Reserve Notes (US Dollars) as legal tender and created a system of private and public entities, with at least eight and no more than twelve regional Federal Reserve banks.
