How has this clause been interpreted and applied by the US Supreme Court over time?
The Commerce Clause has been interpreted and applied by the US Supreme Court in varying ways over time. During the Marshall Court era (1801-1835), the Court took a broad view of the clause, empowering Congress to regulate both intrastate and interstate commerce, as well as non-commerce activities. In contrast, the post-1937 era saw the Court limit the scope of the Commerce Clause, restricting Congress's ability to regulate economic activities under its commerce power.

Additionally, the Import-Export Clause has been applied by the Supreme Court to prevent states and the federal government from imposing tariffs on imports and exports without Congressional consent. The Court has also ruled that the clause only applies to imports and exports, not interstate commerce.

The Copyright Clause has been interpreted by the Supreme Court to grant Congress the power to stimulate the development of works protected by copyright. The Court has defined the limitations of this clause through various cases, determining that its purpose is to encourage the creation of new works, rather than to restrict their use.
