Identify the geographical region of the Ohio Valley and its historical context.
The geographical region of the Ohio Valley is located west of the Appalachian Mountains and north of the upper Ohio River, extending to Lake Erie. It encompasses roughly all of present-day Ohio, northwestern West Virginia, Western Pennsylvania, and a wedge of southeastern Indiana.

Historically, the Ohio Valley was a disputed region in the 17th and 18th centuries, claimed by various Native American tribes, including the Iroquois, and later by France and Great Britain. The region was explored by Christopher Gist in 1750-1753, who surveyed the area and identified lands for potential settlement. The Ohio Valley was also home to various prehistoric cultures, including the Fort Ancient people, who grew beans around 1150-1200 AD.
